**Files changed:** `src/c_grep/c_grep.c` (created from scratch)

**Behavior implemented:**
- Fixed byte-substring search (not regex), case-sensitive, empty pattern matches every line
- Argument parsing: `--` separator, first non-option is PATTERN, rest are file operands; any unsupported `-option` before pattern → diagnostic + exit 2; missing pattern → diagnostic + exit 2
- Line semantics: `\n`-delimited, final unterminated line is a line, every selected line emitted with trailing newline, empty input produces no output
- Binary-safe: NUL, CR, invalid UTF-8 all treated as ordinary data
- File handling: stdin when no operands; `-` is an ordinary pathname; symlinks followed; directories and missing/unreadable files diagnosed by name with processing continuing to next operand
- Multi-file prefix: 2+ file operands → `operand:` prefix on every selected line; 1 file or stdin → no prefix; decision made before searching
- Exit codes: 0 (match, no error), 1 (no match, no error), 2 (any error including usage/operand/alloc/IO)
- Successful stderr is empty; all diagnostics go to stderr
- `search_stream` is the semantic anchor reachable from `main`
- Handles size overflow in buffer growth and all allocation/IO failures without undefined behavior or crashes
- C11 + POSIX.1-2008 only; no external process calls; compiles clean under `-Wall -Wextra -Werror -pedantic`

**Interpretation chosen:** `--` is treated as a one-time option terminator; if it appears after the pattern position, it is treated as a file operand (a normal pathname). This is consistent with "A bare `--` ends option parsing" — once option parsing has ended (pattern found), subsequent tokens are operands.
